  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The equation\n\n$$\n(mx+10)(x+n)=-5{x}^{2}+20x-20\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $m$ and $n$ are constants. What is the value of $m+n?$\n\n$m+n=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the product on the left-hand side must expand to match the right-hand side.\n\nFirst, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(mx+10)(x+n) & =m{x}^{2}+mnx+10x+10n \\\\  & =m{x}^{2}+(mn+10)x+10n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we equate the expanded form to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nm{x}^{2}+(mn+10)x+10n=-5{x}^{2}+20x-20.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms.\n\n- Equating the coefficients of ${x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-5.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n10n=-20\\,\u21d2\\,n=-2.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that values $m=-5$ and $n=-2$ produce the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}mn+10 & =(-5)(-2)+10 \\\\  & =10+10 \\\\  & =20\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the right-hand side. So, our solution is valid.\n\nFinally, we compute the value of $m+n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m+n & =-5+(-2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
